 "sq": [
  {
   "_id": 13243,
   "ANSWER": "<p>Back-pointers maintain a list with each object pointing to all capabilities associated with it. During revocation, the system follows these pointers to change or delete capabilities.<br data-end=\"1027\" data-start=\"1024\" />\n<strong data-end=\"1040\" data-start=\"1027\">Drawback:</strong> Implementation is costly as it requires maintaining and updating pointer lists for all capabilities.</p>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC202J",
   "LEVEL": 3,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2",
    "PLO3",
    "PLO4",
    "PLO13",
    "PLO12"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p>What is <strong data-end=\"800\" data-start=\"772\">back-pointers revocation</strong> and state one drawback?</p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 503,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 13243
  },
  {
   "_id": 13244,
   "ANSWER": "<ul data-end=\"1670\" data-start=\"1327\">\n\t<li data-end=\"1494\" data-start=\"1327\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1494\" data-start=\"1329\"><strong data-end=\"1368\" data-start=\"1329\">DAC (Discretionary Access Control):</strong> Owners set permissions, user-controlled, flexible but vulnerable if user misconfigures or attacker gains owner/root access.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"1670\" data-start=\"1495\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1670\" data-start=\"1497\"><strong data-end=\"1532\" data-start=\"1497\">MAC (Mandatory Access Control):</strong> System-enforced, applies security labels, users cannot modify policies, provides strong security as even root cannot bypass restrictions.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC202J",
   "LEVEL": 4,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO1",
    "PLO2",
    "PLO3",
    "PLO4",
    "PLO13",
    "PLO12"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p>Compare <strong data-end=\"1266\" data-start=\"1251\">DAC and MAC</strong> based on control and security enforcement.</p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 503,
   "TYPE": "sq",
   "key": 13244
  }
